"use strict";(self.webpackChunkreact_native_teleport_docs=self.webpackChunkreact_native_teleport_docs||[]).push([[6936],{8090:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"1-3-0-measurements-rewrite","metadata":{"permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/1-3-0-measurements-rewrite","editUrl":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/tree/main/docs/blog/2026-11-25-measurements-rewrite.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-25-measurements-rewrite.mdx","title":"Version 1.3.0: teleported views always know where they are \ud83e\udded","description":"react-native-teleport 1.3.0 is on its way. This release rewrites how Fabric measures teleported content: instead of keeping a pre-calculated offset in sync with the screen, a teleported Portal now looks up the position of its host directly in the shadow tree.","date":"2026-11-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"react-native","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/react-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"teleport","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/teleport"},{"inline":true,"label":"fabric","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/fabric"},{"inline":true,"label":"shadow-tree","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/shadow-tree"},{"inline":true,"label":"release","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":5.67,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Kirill Zyusko","title":"Library author","url":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko","socials":{"x":"https://x.com/ziusko","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/kiryl-ziusko-9a34a4163/","github":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko.png","key":"kirill","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"1-3-0-measurements-rewrite","title":"Version 1.3.0: teleported views always know where they are \ud83e\udded","authors":["kirill"],"tags":["react-native","teleport","fabric","shadow-tree","release"],"keywords":["react-native-teleport","teleport","portal","fabric","shadow tree","measure","pressable","ScrollView"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"What\'s coming in 1.2.0: smoother teleportation on Android \ud83d\ude80","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/1-2-0-android-fast-reparenting"}},"content":"`react-native-teleport` **1.3.0** is on its way. This release **rewrites how Fabric measures teleported content**: instead of keeping a pre-calculated offset in sync with the screen, a teleported `Portal` now looks up the position of its host directly in the shadow tree.\\n\\nThe result: `measure()` and `Pressable` keep working after you scroll, and a whole class of \\"stale position\\" bugs is gone for good!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## \ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f One view, two addresses\\n\\nWhen you render something through a `Portal`, it lives in **two places at once**:\\n\\n- in the **React** tree (and in Fabric\'s **shadow tree**, which mirrors it) it\'s still a child of the `Portal`. This is what keeps context, state and props working;\\n- on the **screen** (the native view hierarchy) it\'s a child of the `PortalHost`. This is where it\'s **actually drawn**.\\n\\nMost of the time nobody cares about this difference. But sometimes React Native asks: _\\"where exactly on the screen is this view?\\"_. `measure()` asks it, and `Pressable` asks it **all the time** while you hold your finger on a button: if the finger moves too far away from the button, the press gets cancelled.\\n\\nTo answer this question Fabric walks **up the shadow tree** and adds up the positions of all parents. And in the shadow tree our button still sits inside the `Portal`, at its **old place**. So without any help Fabric points at the **wrong spot**.\\n\\n## \ud83c\udfe0 The old way: \\"go to my old house and walk 3 blocks\\"\\n\\nSince `1.1.10` ([#150](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/pull/150)) we helped Fabric with a correction. The native side measured where the host and the source are on the screen and stored the difference between them (`host \u2212 source`) as an offset in the portal state. Fabric then calculated the position as:\\n\\n```text\\nposition = Portal (shadow tree) + offset (native)\\n```\\n\\nThink of it as directions like _\\"go to my old house, then walk 3 blocks east\\"_. It works perfectly - as long as **the old house doesn\'t move**.\\n\\nBut it can! Declare a `Portal` inside a `ScrollView` and scroll it: the first part of the formula changes (Fabric knows about the scroll), while the second part stays the same (scrolling doesn\'t trigger any layout callback on the native side, so nobody recalculates the \\"3 blocks\\"). Now the answer is off by exactly the scroll distance, and `Pressable` cancels your press as soon as the finger moves a little ([#220](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/issues/220)):\\n\\nimport ComparisonTable from \\"@site/src/components/ComparisonTable\\";\\nimport scrolledSourceBefore from \\"@site/static/img/measurements-rewrite/scrolled-source-before.png\\";\\nimport scrolledSourceAfter from \\"@site/static/img/measurements-rewrite/scrolled-source-after.png\\";\\n\\n<ComparisonTable\\n  left={\\n    <img\\n      width={280}\\n      alt=\\"1.2.5: after scrolling the source by 240 the measured position is off by 240 and the press is cancelled\\"\\n      src={scrolledSourceBefore}\\n    />\\n  }\\n  right={\\n    <img\\n      width={280}\\n      alt=\\"1.3.0: after scrolling the source by 240 the measured position matches the actual one and the press is counted\\"\\n      src={scrolledSourceAfter}\\n    />\\n  }\\n  leftText={\\n    <i>\\n      1.2.5: the measured position is off by the scroll distance (240), the\\n      second press is cancelled\\n    </i>\\n  }\\n  rightText={\\n    <i>\\n      1.3.0: the measured position matches the actual one, the press is counted\\n    </i>\\n  }\\n/>\\n\\nThe two halves of the formula come from **two different worlds** that get updated at **different moments**. We had already fixed another case where these two parts drifted apart ([#172](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/pull/172)), and every such fix meant one more native hook whose only job was to keep two numbers in sync. Recalculating the offset on every frame would have worked too, but then every teleported view would cost you work on **every frame**, even when nothing moves.\\n\\n## \ud83e\udded The new way: just ask where the host is\\n\\nIn `1.3.0` we stopped patching the formula and **removed the source from it entirely**.\\n\\nA teleported `Portal` is now marked as a **root node** in the shadow tree (the `RootNodeKind` trait). This is the same trick React Native uses for `<Modal>` and `react-native-screens` uses for `FullWindowOverlay` - both have the same _\\"lives here, drawn there\\"_ problem. When Fabric measures a view and reaches such a node, it **stops walking up**.\\n\\nAt that point the `Portal` tells Fabric where to continue from: the **position of the host**, read from **the same revision of the shadow tree** that is being measured.\\n\\n```text\\nposition = host (shadow tree)\\n```\\n\\nBack to the analogy: instead of _\\"old house + 3 blocks\\"_ you now hand out the **real address**. Scroll the source, scroll the host, transform, rotate, re-layout - the answer is calculated at the moment of the question, from the data Fabric already has.\\n\\n## \u2728 Why it\'s better\\n\\n- **Nothing can get stale.** There is no cached offset anymore, so there is nothing to keep in sync. Scrolling the source and scrolling the host are fixed by the same change.\\n- **Nothing runs per frame.** The position is calculated only when somebody asks for it.\\n- **Less native code.** The native side now reports only _which_ host the children are mounted into and its size. The screen offset calculations and the layout and attachment hooks on iOS and Android are gone.\\n- **One source of truth.** Layout, transforms and scroll offsets of the host\'s ancestors are taken exactly as Fabric knows them. A new way of moving something on the screen is covered automatically, instead of waiting for its own bug report and its own fix.\\n\\nOn top of that, `1.3.0` removes a one-frame flash on Android when a `Portal` switches to another host ([#228](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/issues/228)): the `Portal` now takes the size of the new host in the same commit that changes `hostName`, instead of drawing one frame with the layout of the previous host.\\n\\n## \ud83d\udce6 Why `1.3.0` and not `1.2.6`?\\n\\nThere are **no API changes** - your code stays exactly the same. But the way the library is linked into React Native **has changed**:\\n\\n- to read the shadow tree the library now uses Fabric\'s `UIManager` (through `ShadowTreeRevisionProvider`) and `RuntimeScheduler`. Everything it needs is available starting from **React Native 0.78**, so this is the new **minimum supported version**;\\n- on iOS the library depends on more React Native pods. With `use_frameworks!` and React Native built from source, the podspec now declares `React-runtimescheduler`, `React-performancetimeline` and (up to React Native 0.81) `React-jsinspectortracing` for you.\\n\\nSince this can affect your build, it deserves a minor version rather than a patch that you might pull in without noticing.\\n\\n## \ud83d\ude80 Upgrading\\n\\n```sh\\nyarn add react-native-teleport@1.3.0\\ncd ios && pod install\\n```\\n\\n:::info React-native < 0.78\\nIf you are on React Native older than 0.78 - stay on 1.2.x until you upgrade React Native.\\n:::\\n\\nIf you notice a wrong position, a cancelled press or a build issue after upgrading, please [open an issue](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/issues) with your React Native version and a reproduction."},{"id":"1-2-0-android-fast-reparenting","metadata":{"permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/1-2-0-android-fast-reparenting","editUrl":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/tree/main/docs/blog/2026-08-23-android-fast-reparenting.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-23-android-fast-reparenting.mdx","title":"What\'s coming in 1.2.0: smoother teleportation on Android \ud83d\ude80","description":"react-native-teleport 1.2.0 is just around the corner. This release focuses on one major improvement: a reworked reparenting mechanism on Android.","date":"2026-08-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"react-native","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/react-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"android","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/android"},{"inline":true,"label":"teleport","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/teleport"},{"inline":true,"label":"reparenting","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/reparenting"},{"inline":true,"label":"release","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":2.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Kirill Zyusko","title":"Library author","url":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko","socials":{"x":"https://x.com/ziusko","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/kiryl-ziusko-9a34a4163/","github":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko.png","key":"kirill","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"1-2-0-android-fast-reparenting","title":"What\'s coming in 1.2.0: smoother teleportation on Android \ud83d\ude80","authors":["kirill"],"tags":["react-native","android","teleport","reparenting","release"],"keywords":["react-native-teleport","android","teleport","reparenting","fast reparenting","surface-view","texture-view"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Version 1.3.0: teleported views always know where they are \ud83e\udded","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/1-3-0-measurements-rewrite"},"nextItem":{"title":"Illusion of portals in React Native \ud83d\udef8","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/illusion-of-portals"}},"content":"`react-native-teleport` **1.2.0** is just around the corner. This release focuses on one major improvement: a **reworked reparenting** mechanism on **Android**.\\n\\nWith the new fast path, you should see in most cases no empty frames while teleporting views. The difference is especially noticeable for surface-backed content such as videos and maps.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## \u2b1c Why did empty frames appear?\\n\\nTeleport moves an existing native view between parents without remounting the React component. On Android, that move previously used the regular `removeView()` and `addView()` APIs.\\n\\nAlthough the same React component and native view were preserved, Android temporarily detached the view from the window. For content backed by a `SurfaceView` or `TextureView`, that could disconnect or recreate the rendering surface. While Android waited for the next frame, the background underneath became visible as a black, white, or otherwise empty flash.\\n\\nThis was easier to spot on older devices, but it could also happen intermittently on newer versions of Android.\\n\\n## \ud83d\ude80 A faster reparenting path\\n\\nIn 1.2.0, Teleport can move an already attached view using Android\'s lightweight `detachViewFromParent()` and `attachViewToParent()` APIs.\\n\\nThe view is removed from one parent\'s child list and inserted into the other **synchronously, without leaving the Android window**. This keeps its rendering resources alive and lets the compositor continue displaying the previously submitted frame throughout the move.\\n\\nIn practice, teleporting videos, maps, and other surface-backed views should now feel much more seamless.\\n\\nimport ComparisonTable from \\"@site/src/components/ComparisonTable\\";\\nimport Video from \\"@site/src/components/Video\\";\\n\\n<ComparisonTable\\n  left={\\n    <Video\\n      width={50}\\n      aria-label=\\"Android teleportation with regular reparenting\\"\\n      src=\\"/video/fast-reparenting-before.mp4\\"\\n    />\\n  }\\n  right={\\n    <Video\\n      width={51}\\n      aria-label=\\"Android teleportation with the fast reparenting path\\"\\n      src=\\"/video/fast-reparenting-after.mp4\\"\\n    />\\n  }\\n  leftText={<i>Video is flickering while teleporting</i>}\\n  rightText={<i>Video gets teleported without flickering</i>}\\n/>\\n\\n## \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f Safe by default\\n\\nThe fast path is used only when Teleport can safely preserve the view\'s window attachment - for example, when both parents belong to the same Android window and no active focus or layout transition requires the full detach lifecycle.\\n\\nWhen those conditions are not met, Teleport automatically falls back to the existing `removeView()` and `addView()` behavior. There is no new API or configuration to adopt: the optimization happens internally.\\n\\n## \ud83e\uddea Tested against empty frames\\n\\nAlongside the new mechanism, we added an end-to-end regression test that records a video teleportation and checks the result for empty frames. This gives us a way to catch the original visual glitch automatically before it reaches another release.\\n\\nThe fast path has also been tested with video and map content across Android API levels 28, 31, and 36.\\n\\n## \u2728 Upgrading\\n\\nThere are no breaking API changes in 1.2.0. Upgrade as usual:\\n\\n```sh\\nyarn add react-native-teleport@1.2.0\\n```\\n\\nThat\'s it - existing teleports will automatically use the new Android fast path whenever it is safe to do so.\\n\\nIf you still notice an empty frame after upgrading, please [open an issue](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/issues) with your device, Android version, and the type of view being teleported."},{"id":"illusion-of-portals","metadata":{"permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/illusion-of-portals","editUrl":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/tree/main/docs/blog/2026-06-23-illusion-of-portals.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-23-illusion-of-portals.mdx","title":"Illusion of portals in React Native \ud83d\udef8","description":"Ever wondered what really happens when you render something through a portal in React Native?","date":"2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"react-native","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/react-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"portal","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/portal"},{"inline":true,"label":"teleport","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/teleport"},{"inline":true,"label":"reparenting","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/reparenting"}],"readingTime":0.59,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Kirill Zyusko","title":"Library author","url":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko","socials":{"x":"https://x.com/ziusko","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/kiryl-ziusko-9a34a4163/","github":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko.png","key":"kirill","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"illusion-of-portals","title":"Illusion of portals in React Native \ud83d\udef8","authors":["kirill"],"tags":["react-native","portal","teleport","reparenting"],"keywords":["react-native-teleport","portal","teleport","teleported","reparent","reparenting","re-parent"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"What\'s coming in 1.2.0: smoother teleportation on Android \ud83d\ude80","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/1-2-0-android-fast-reparenting"},"nextItem":{"title":"Meet teleport!","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/welcome"}},"content":"**Ever wondered what really happens when you render something through a portal in React Native?**\\n\\nIn my talk **The Illusion of Portals in React Native** at [App.js Conf](https://appjs.co/), we look beyond the familiar **Portal** API to understand how different implementations actually work, how they affect the React tree, and what a true portal should look like internally \u2014 from the illusion created at the React layer to native portals, view re-parenting, and the new possibilities they unlock.\\n\\nStep through the portal and join me for the ride!\\n\\n<iframe\\n  width=\\"560\\"\\n  height=\\"315\\"\\n  src=\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/w90acmyrTXk\\"\\n  title=\\"Illusion of Portals\\"\\n  frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n  allow=\\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\\"\\n  allowFullScreen\\n/>"},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport/tree/main/docs/blog/2026-01-30-first-stable-release.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-30-first-stable-release.mdx","title":"Meet teleport!","description":"I\'m excited to announce the first stable release of react-native-teleport! \ud83c\udf89","date":"2026-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"portal","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/portal"},{"inline":true,"label":"teleport","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/teleport"},{"inline":true,"label":"react-native","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/tags/react-native"}],"readingTime":3.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Kirill Zyusko","title":"Library author","url":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko","socials":{"x":"https://x.com/ziusko","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/kiryl-ziusko-9a34a4163/","github":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/kirillzyusko.png","key":"kirill","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Meet teleport!","authors":["kirill"],"tags":["portal","teleport","react-native"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Illusion of portals in React Native \ud83d\udef8","permalink":"/react-native-teleport/pr-preview/pr-230/blog/illusion-of-portals"}},"content":"I\'m excited to announce the **first stable release of `react-native-teleport`**! \ud83c\udf89\\n\\nAfter extensive development, community feedback and integrating the lib into many prod apps, we\'ve reached a big milestone: a **production-ready** portal solution that brings **true native portals** to react-native \u2014 something the framework has been missing for years.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is `react-native-teleport`?\\n\\n`react-native-teleport` solves a fundamental problem in React Native development: it brings missing [Portal](https://react.dev/reference/react-dom/createPortal) component to react-native.\\n\\nHistorically, developers had to use JS based solutions to render modals, tooltips, overlays, and other components that need to visually appear in a different part of your UI tree. While they work, they have significant limitation: they **physically** render components in a **different part of React tree**, **breaking context** and bringing other related problems.\\n\\nThe `react-native-teleport` solves this problem by moving rendering of components in the native layer. This library provides a **declarative API**, doesn\'t rely on **_internal_** React implementation details, and **works identically** on **iOS, Android, and Web**.\\n\\n## Not only Portal\\n\\nAs you may noticed from the library title this library is **not only a portal solution**. It also provides a **re-parenting pattern** that allows you to **move existing components without unmounting or remounting** them.\\n\\nWith that technique you can build **advanced and seamless** transitions, implement **shared-element-style** transitions between screens, and more.\\n\\nFor example you can implement photos app transitions like this without flickering, white/black screens, jumps and other distracting things:\\n\\nimport Video from \\"@site/src/components/Video\\";\\n\\n<Video src=\\"/video/photos.mp4\\" width={35} />\\n<br />\\n\\nThe great thing is that view is **not re-created or destroyed** when it\'s moved between different parents. This mean you can use this lib for stateful components, like `Video`/`Lottie` etc. and you can easily replicate patterns that you\'ve seen in many well-known apps, for example you can re-build Instagram-like reels transition:\\n\\n<Video src=\\"/video/instagram.mp4\\" width={35} />\\n<br />\\n\\n## Not only about transitions and rendering outside\\n\\nTraditionally, React has three operations in its reconciliation mechanism: **create**, **update**, and **destroy**. When you **move** a component from one parent to another in the React tree, it gets **destroyed** and **recreated**.\\n\\nBut effectively `react-native-teleport` **unlocks a new dimension** and introduces a fourth operation: **move**. This is a game-changer because it allows you to:\\n\\n### Pre-render Components Offscreen\\n\\nYou can render expensive components off-screen and move them into view only when needed. This is particularly useful for complex overlays, modals, or bottom sheets that require significant setup time.\\n\\n```tsx\\n// Pre-render the editor off-screen\\n<Portal hostName={used ? \\"editor\\" : undefined}>\\n  <ExpensiveRichTextEditor />\\n</Portal>\\n\\n// Later, when user triggers an action, simply move it into view\\n// No re-rendering, no state loss, just a native view reposition\\n```\\n\\n### Re-use Views Between Screens\\n\\nOne of the most powerful patterns enabled by the \\"**move**\\" operation is **view reuse across navigation transitions**. You can render the same component instance on multiple screens and seamlessly move it as users navigate.\\n\\nImagine you have the same component rendered twice on two different screens. If it\'s heavy for rendering a typical path is to dig into performance bottlenecks, fixing them etc. But with `react-native-teleport` you can **simply move** the component between screens \ud83e\udd2f\\n\\n### And more\\n\\nI\'m still exploring of what can be done with this library. Things that I discovered helped to build flicker free transitions while moving heavy views and helped to optimize performance from the angle that I never thought about (just re-using the view without re-creating it).\\n\\nBut I continue to explore what other patterns and use cases can be enabled with this library. I\'m also looking for feedback from the community and I\'m open to suggestions and ideas.\\n\\n## What\'s Next?\\n\\nI have exciting plans for future releases:\\n\\n- \ud83e\ude9e Mirror support for advanced mirroring patterns\\n- \ud83d\ude80 Performance optimizations\\n- \ud83e\udd13 Additional platform support\\n- \ud83d\ude4c Community-driven features based on feedback\\n\\n## Thank You\\n\\nThis milestone wouldn\'t be possible without the incredible react-native community. Thank you to everyone who tested, provided feedback, reported issues, and contributed to making `react-native-teleport` better.\\n\\nIf you find this library useful, please consider:\\n\\n- \u2b50 Starring on [GitHub](https://github.com/kirillzyusko/react-native-teleport)\\n- \ud83d\udce2 Sharing with the community\\n- \ud83d\udc1b Reporting issues and providing feedback\\n- \ud83e\udd1d Contributing improvements"}]}}')}}]);